WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.990
“No one has ever done that, no one has ever
done that in the history of Dota.”

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.200
What sort of play could possibly have caused
such a reaction?

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.380
What sort of play could be worthy of such
praise?

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.500
It was bold.

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:15.800
It was innovative.

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:19.080
It was completely and utterly insane.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.480
This is No Tidehunter’s Roshan Bait from
DreamHack Winter 2012.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:33.220
It’s the Grand Finals of DreamHack Winter
2012. An unsponsored local team, No Tidehunter

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.520
were up against the American powerhouse, Evil
Geniuses.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.630
Against all odds, No Tidehunter not only topped
their group, but made it all the way to the

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.320
grand finals without dropping a single map.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:52.660
(Casting)

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:59.000
Led by seasoned veterans Loda and Akke, the
team was mostly made up of inexperienced players,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.239
fielding the likes of s4, AdmiralBulldog and
EternalEnVy.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:08.970
In true David vs. Goliath fashion, their opponent,
Evil Geniuses, were a titan of the Dota scene

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:13.200
that featured establish stars like Maelk,
DeMoN and Fear.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:20.760
Fear: "The competition this year, it's really insane actually because, the way I see it, no one that I know of can predict the win."

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.400
On paper, No Tidehunter shouldn’t have given
EG too much trouble, and Game 1 seemed to

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.690
follow that script.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:32.720
After EG lost their group stage match against
the new squad, they seemed to have figured

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.680
out the Swedes’ playstyle, making short
work of them in the rematch.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:54.840
(Casting)

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.860
Following their decisive 35 minute win, Evil
Geniuses appeared poised to take the title

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.310
and shut the door on No Tidehunter’s underdog
story.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.739
The Swedes needed to come up with something
to shift the momentum back into their favor

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:10.580
and they needed it soon.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:15.400
After all, necessity is the mother of all
invention, but no one could have expected

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.440
what came next.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:03:04.440
(Casting)

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.480
With TobiWan’s now legendary words echoing
through the venue, No Tidehunter pulled off

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:14.099
what is now known as “the Roshan Bait,”
rocketing them to an early lead and sending

00:03:14.099 --> 00:03:15.819
the crowd to their feet.

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:21.120
To this day, that play is still one of the
most clever and most unique plays in Dota’s

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.240
lengthy history.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.440
S4: "To start off we were trying to do a level one Rosh but then we came up with better ideas as it went on."

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:33.580
Using AdmiralBulldog’s intentional suicide
as bait, NTH convinced EG that they were attempting

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.640
to do a sneaky Level 1 Rosh.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.800
S4: "We played the Grand Finals, not on the main stage Game 1.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.680
And then Game 2 we had to go onto the main stage but we had a 30 minute break.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.080
So, it's like, what are we going to do guys? We're down one game

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.200
we're playing against EG.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.160
So we just came up with a strat to get some momentum, you know, get into the game"

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.920
With two early kills under their belts
and EG’s momentum waned, The Roshan Bait

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.560
put NTH in prime position to reclaim control
of the series and they made sure to jump on

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.530
the opportunity.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.660
Hoisting the trophy high above their heads,
No Tidehunter secured their place in Dota

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:15.540
history by not only winning the title at a
Major event despite not having a sponsor,

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.930
but for doing so while pulling off one of
the most creative and effective tricks in

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:21.360
esports history.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.120
While new, inventive strategies are continually
pushing the boundaries, few can match this one.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.920
NTH had the audacity to pull-off the bait
in the grand finals against a veteran team

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.320
and their execution was flawless.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.680
(Casting)

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.600
It might have happened really early in Dota 2's history

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.760
but the Roshan Bait still stands
as one of the game’s most memorable moments.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.440
Time has passed, other plays have come and
gone, but something about the Roshan Bait

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.640
has caused it to remain.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.720
(Casting)

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.600
Maybe it's Tobiwan's perfect call.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:07.600
Maybe it's the underdogs pulling off an unlikely victory against a perennial powerhouse.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.840
Or maybe it’s that, for one incredible moment,
No Tidehunter proved that the only

00:05:12.849 --> 00:05:15.619
limit to what is possible is your creativity.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:21.640
(Casting)

